<p>The United States is reviewing ways to penalize NATO allies that, according to a Reuters report on an internal Pentagon email, did not back Washington strongly enough over Iran. Spain has emerged as the main focus after possible measures reportedly included suspending it from the alliance. NATO responded that its founding treaty contains “no provision” for suspending or expelling a member, a sign that any such move would face major legal and political obstacles. European allies pushed back quickly. Spain said it remains a “reliable” NATO member, and Britain also rejected reported US plans to punish countries that Washington sees as having offered too little support. The episode has sharpened tensions inside the alliance at a moment when European governments are trying to preserve NATO unity, even as they differ with President Trump over Iran and over what alliance solidarity should look like.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/r6PBsIVpa9cK_ECh4XRObxLAlpfuKV7ArbeSXRk-FAS-ufx6JUOKXeodcvzVunqrHWLbQFScwrspSm0uXXiw4EiPpz3rUvbxXrniJMVX3APA3yNGh8f7KAD7Pu7h5UodcqWq4r_NNBbWWJvaiu4RBzajoeW0oysAfkz9PbNA-yRlYr18f-WJ51RPIozKYC9zS9KBnURYeAQLSX9EhruAH-XE0uwBkg' alt='Prime Minister Keir Starmer of Britain in London on Wednesday. An internal Pentagon email suggested that America could withdraw support for Britain’s sovereignty over the Falkland Islands.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Treaty limits: NATO officials said the alliance’s rules include no mechanism to suspend or expel members, undercutting the most severe option reportedly discussed in the Pentagon email.</li><li>Britain named: The New York Times reported that Britain was also part of the review, with one option involving US support for British sovereignty over the Falkland Islands.</li><li>Europe reacts: BBC analysis said European NATO governments were alarmed by the report and moved to defend Spain, reflecting broader concern about another rupture in transatlantic relations.</li><li>Madrid response: Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez said Spain is a dependable ally after the report surfaced, aiming to reassure partners and calm speculation about its standing in NATO.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NATO: The alliance said its treaty has no provision for suspending or expelling a member state, signaling institutional resistance to the idea raised in the reported Pentagon review. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cz78x703lrvo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>Spanish government: Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez defended Spain’s record and described the country as a reliable NATO ally after reports that Washington was considering punitive steps. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/spain-is-reliable-nato-member-pm-says-after-reported-us-ouster-threat'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>British government: Britain rejected reported plans by President Trump to punish it for giving too little support over Iran, alongside Spain’s pushback. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/24/world/europe/trump-britain-spain-falklands.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>European allies: According to BBC analysis, European NATO members rallied around Spain and saw the report as another sign of worsening US-Europe relations inside the alliance. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cj40r2gw24wo'>BBC News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/639028-us-spain-nato-suspension/'>US considers suspending Spain from NATO – Reuters</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cz78x703lrvo'>Nato says &#x27;no provision&#x27; to expel members after report US could seek to suspend Spain</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/24/world/europe/trump-britain-spain-falklands.html'>Britain and Spain Reject Reported Plans by Trump to Punish Them</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://novaramedia.com/2026/04/24/trump-threatens-to-expel-spain-from-nato-over-iran-war/'>Trump Threatens to Expel Spain From NATO Over Iran War</a> - novaramedia.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/spain-is-reliable-nato-member-pm-says-after-reported-us-ouster-threat'>Spain is ‘reliable’ NATO member, PM says after reported US ouster threat</a> - straitstimes.com</li></ul>